{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785868565,"nonce":"GiQuVKtejrgrGlLfSI0WSVTFKy8=","message":"Implementation gotchas for the proposed fix:\n\n1. The commented-out unwrap regex was disabled in 084f397 (2020) because\n   it ran ungated over entire message bodies, including follow-up emails,\n   where it merged unrelated prose lines. Any revival must at minimum be\n   gated on was_trailer and restricted to followup=False (self-authored\n   commit messages), never follow-up parsing.\n\n2. Bracket-matching heuristics are inherently fragile. A \"first line has\n   `[` but no `]`\" trigger fails on annotations that contain bracketed\n   references, e.g.:\n\n       Reviewed-by: Alex Maintainer \u003calex@foo.org\u003e\n       [Only for the case that is not specifically covered by the\n       discussion [1] and excluding subsystem bar]\n\n   The [1] puts a `]` on the first line and the accumulation never\n   starts. Bracket-depth counting handles this but then breaks on\n   unbalanced brackets in prose (smileys, \"[sic\", quoted ranges).\n   Every refinement just relocates the failure mode; do not go down\n   this road.\n\n3. Preferred direction: stop trying to classify the comment syntax and\n   instead scope by structure, the way git interpret-trailers does.\n   Within the final trailer block (last paragraph of the message), keep\n   any non-trailer, non-blank line positionally attached to the trailer\n   above it as opaque extinfo, regardless of its syntax. The question\n   changes from \"is this a well-formed bracket comment?\" (unanswerable)\n   to \"is this inside the trailer block?\" (structural).\n\n4. Residual risk of the structural approach: a prose paragraph butted\n   directly against the trailer block with no separating blank line\n   would now stick to the trailers instead of moving up. Rare in\n   self-authored commits, and arguably today's behavior is no more\n   correct there.\n\n5. Regardless of the parsing fix, b4 send should warn when a rewrite\n   would relocate non-trailer lines out of the trailer block. The\n   silent mangling is the core complaint in the report; converting it\n   into a visible diff/warning is valuable even on its own.\n\n6. Multi-line extinfo must round-trip through LoreTrailer.as_string()\n   and rebuild_message() without re-wrapping or flattening; add tests\n   for that specifically.","files":null}]}